1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
So we've been going over adverse selection.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,320
And this was one of the most technical economic concepts

3
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:18,760
to ever make it into national media.

4
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:21,400
So during the Obamacare debates, this

5
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,200
was actually discussed quite widely by op-ed columnists

6
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:31,040
and so on, usually combining with moral hazard too.

7
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:33,360
Now there's one huge problem here,

8
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,880
which is, is there anything at all

9
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,200
about giving people free health care that

10
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:40,840
solves moral hazard problems?

11
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:42,240
No.

12
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:42,920
Let's see.

13
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:45,320
Is there any way in which giving people free health care

14
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,360
increases moral hazard problems?

15
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:48,600
Yes.

16
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:49,120
Right?

17
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:52,200
So with private insurance, your rates

18
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:55,040
will reflect risks, which leads to some incentive

19
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:59,280
to not have bad unhealthy behavior, right?

20
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:01,640
Because if you smoke, then this will raise your rates.

21
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:03,720
But if everybody gets free health care,

22
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:06,560
what does this do to the incentive to smoke?

23
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:07,160
Increases.

24
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:08,840
Increases the incentive to smoke.

25
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,240
What about the incentive for mountain climbing?

26
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:11,740
Increases.

27
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:14,080
Yes, incentive for obesity.

28
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:14,760
Increases.

29
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:17,640
So it's completely bizarre that someone

30
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:21,480
would pair these two concepts as if they both

31
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:24,040
argue for Obamacare.

32
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:25,720
But yet, you're like, why?

33
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:29,760
Why would someone just mention both concepts

34
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:34,880
when the first one is clearly an argument on the other side?

35
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,400
Most people don't have any patience

36
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:37,880
to find out what the concepts mean,

37
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:39,880
so they're just looking for some slogans.

38
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:42,840
Anyway, so moral hazard is clearly

39
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:46,800
an argument against giving everyone free health care.

40
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:49,080
And in general, it's an argument against giving people

41
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:51,080
the same rates regardless of the risk.

42
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:53,080
Because when you give people the same rates

43
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:54,840
regardless of the risk, this encourages

44
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:56,160
people to take more risks.

45
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:57,160
Of course.

46
00:01:57,160 --> 00:01:59,880
What else could it do?

47
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:02,680
Which is why I had a post where I talked about moral selection

48
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:05,440
and adverse hazard, whatever.

49
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:08,840
Not even really interested in what the concepts say,

50
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:12,440
except insofar as they are rationalization for a policy

51
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:15,480
that people would have favored regardless of the arguments.

52
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:18,200
But anyway, that's what I really think.

53
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:20,000
That, that's what I really think.

54
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:21,720
So moral hazard, verified.

55
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:26,480
Adverse selection, at least there is a clear connection.

56
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,440
Just like in that problem that I gave you

57
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:31,960
where you've got these companies where the value goes from 0

58
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:34,960
to 100, but the current owner of the company

59
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:37,360
knows the true value, whereas the buyer only

60
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:39,360
knows the average value of the company's theory.

61
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:42,080
So like I said, in this case, there

62
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:45,440
is actually a social gain of forcing everyone

63
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:47,520
to go and sell companies.

64
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:50,080
Because remember, we talked about what's the equilibrium

65
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:52,640
price of the simple model.

66
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:55,520
What's the equilibrium price of the simple model?

67
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:56,080
0.

68
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:58,680
0 is the equilibrium price of the simple model.

69
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:00,960
And therefore, what surplus is realized

70
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:02,720
in the simple version of the model?

71
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:04,560
No surplus.

72
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:08,520
On the other hand, if you force everybody to sell,

73
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:12,400
the result is going to be that we realize a surplus.

74
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:14,160
That's the average value of 50.

75
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,520
And the average social gain of a sale is 50%.

76
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:26,000
So we realize an average gain of 25 for a company sold.

77
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,920
So if there's a regulation forcing everyone to sell,

78
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:32,920
this means that everybody sells, of course.

79
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:35,200
We're assuming harsh punishment if you don't sell,

80
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:36,600
so everybody does it.

81
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:41,280
And then the average company sold yields a social gain of 25.

82
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:45,320
And that's a case where this regulation is, in the model,

83
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:46,860
at least, a solution to the problem.

84
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:50,200
It's actually relevant.

85
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:52,520
And again, so the flip side, of course,

86
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:55,000
is if you have an adverse selection problem with health

87
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,920
insurance, if you force everyone to buy,

88
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:02,480
then this means that the low-risk people buy,

89
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:04,160
as well as high-risk people.

90
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:07,280
And again, what happens is that in the extreme case,

91
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:10,680
you go from a market where there is no insurance offered at all,

92
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:12,640
the adverse selection problem is severe enough,

93
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:14,680
to one where everybody gets coverage.

94
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:17,480
Now, the healthy people still are going to be getting

95
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:20,360
ripped off, conventionally, because they would like

96
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:22,000
to drop out of the market.

97
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,320
But again, here, we are at least doing Calgary Hicks analysis.

98
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:29,960
So the idea is that if the numbers work out correctly,

99
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:32,520
then the losses to the healthy people who

100
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:36,240
are buying insurance that is worth less than the cost

101
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:39,400
of them, their losses are going to be more than balanced out

102
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:42,000
by the gains to all of the sick people

103
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,440
who get insurance at a lower price than the value.

104
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:47,320
That's the idea.

105
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:50,520
Now, Krugman, when discussing this,

106
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:52,600
he maintained his economic clarity.

107
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:55,960
And so he actually said something that's very sensible,

108
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:59,720
namely, he said, look, if you study public opinion polls

109
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:02,560
on Obamacare, the one part that's really unpopular

110
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:04,800
is the individual mandate.

111
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:07,080
And interestingly, that's also the only part

112
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:09,560
that really has anything to do with adverse selection.

113
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:13,520
So the one part, so now we also, and then Krugman

114
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:16,680
had a very nice analogy where he said, well, look,

115
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:18,080
programs like Obamacare, and this

116
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:21,640
would also hold true for the other famous predecessor

117
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:24,720
of Obamacare in the state of Massachusetts.

118
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:25,680
Obamacare.

119
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:28,360
Yes, which is extremely similar.

120
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:29,640
Missouri, right?

121
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:32,600
Or maybe not Missouri, if you believe the mean voter model.

122
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:34,760
It's not surprising that both candidates that

123
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:37,840
make it up to the national race are associated

124
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:39,400
with almost the same policies.

125
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:41,560
And in fact, the same in their advisors.

126
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:44,880
Jonathan Gruber was the advisor for both Romney and Massachusetts

127
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:47,600
and Obama at the national level.

128
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:48,400
All right.

129
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:50,400
But we should all praise Jonathan Gruber

130
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:52,200
because he's written a graphic novel.

131
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:54,760
And economists should do this.

132
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:57,120
And it shouldn't be nice, it has to be first.

133
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:59,200
All right, so what Krugman said is

134
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:05,160
that the three main regulations of Obamacare

135
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:07,320
are like the three legs of a stool.

136
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:09,400
And if you don't have all three legs,

137
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:11,440
the stool will be wobbly.

138
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:13,760
And again, so what are the legs?

139
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:17,320
All right, so first of all, you've got the popular stuff.

140
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:20,520
All right, so you've got ban pre-existing conditions

141
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:21,080
clauses.

142
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:27,360
All right, so these are regulations

143
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:30,720
that say that the insurance policy will not

144
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:32,760
cover you for problems that you have at the point

145
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:34,920
you buy the insurance.

146
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:37,720
And in terms of just the logic of insurance,

147
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:43,000
why would firms want to have pre-existing conditions

148
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,000
clauses?

149
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,760
Then everyone will wait to buy insurance.

150
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:48,000
Yeah, you wait to buy insurance until you're sick.

151
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,840
And then it's not really insurance, is it?

152
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:52,200
Then it's just like, give me some free stuff.

153
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:53,320
All right.

154
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:54,780
And of course, insurance companies

155
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:57,480
say, sure, I'd love to give you free stuff for 10% more

156
00:06:57,480 --> 00:06:59,960
than it costs.

157
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:02,480
But that doesn't do what I want.

158
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:03,920
I should have bought it before.

159
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:05,480
But I didn't want to buy it before.

160
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:07,880
All right, so you've got the ban on pre-existing conditions

161
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:09,320
clauses.

162
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:11,120
And let's see, remind me, is that one

163
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:14,480
popular with normal humans or unpopular with normal humans?

164
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:16,360
Very popular with normal humans.

165
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:17,960
Just big smiley face.

166
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:19,120
Yay.

167
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:20,360
All right, yes.

168
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:23,120
Greedy insurance companies shouldn't go and charge people

169
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,560
more just because they already have a problem.

170
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:29,280
They should just give them stuff that let them off.

171
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:31,400
I don't want to open up a business that does this,

172
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:34,080
but the fat cats ought to do this.

173
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:36,560
All right, so you've got the ban on pre-existing conditions

174
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:37,880
clauses.

175
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:41,600
And let's see, then, hold on, let's see.

176
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:43,840
There's, right, so you've got that part.

177
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:44,760
Oh, yeah, there it is.

178
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:47,280
And then you've got something that

179
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:49,200
has actual to do with adverse selection, which

180
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:53,120
is the mandate.

181
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,440
How do people feel about the mandate, generally?

182
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:55,940
Hate it.

183
00:07:55,940 --> 00:07:56,440
Bad.

184
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:56,940
Bad.

185
00:07:56,940 --> 00:07:57,920
Mandate bad?

186
00:07:57,920 --> 00:07:59,080
No.

187
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:01,120
People should get to do whatever they want.

188
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,680
And the other way, you shouldn't have to go and buy something

189
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:06,200
from fat cats, right?

190
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:10,280
And then we've got the last leg, which is the subsidies.

191
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:16,920
And again, normally, so subsidies also popular.

192
00:08:16,920 --> 00:08:18,160
Government's helping you.

193
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:20,240
So good.

194
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,480
All right, now what Krugman points out

195
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:25,040
is that there's a logical relationship between these three

196
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:27,200
essential regulations.

197
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:32,080
Namely, so first of all, so you ban the pre-existing clauses.

198
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:33,640
But then there's the problem of people

199
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:35,480
who won't want to buy the insurance anymore

200
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:36,920
until they have the problem.

201
00:08:36,920 --> 00:08:38,640
And then which of the regulations

202
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:40,240
tries to solve the problem you caused

203
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:42,680
by banning pre-existing conditions clauses?

204
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:43,360
The mandate.

205
00:08:43,360 --> 00:08:44,400
The mandate.

206
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:46,720
And then what regulation solves the problem

207
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:48,960
of all the people who can't afford to buy the mandate

208
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:49,760
insurance?

209
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:51,000
Subsidies.

210
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,320
All right, so Krugman had the sense to say, look,

211
00:08:54,320 --> 00:08:56,880
these regulations all work as a package.

212
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:58,540
And if you just get rid of one of them,

213
00:08:58,540 --> 00:09:00,920
then Obamacare really will be a disaster

214
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:03,680
that critics are predicting.

215
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:05,160
All right, so that's the idea.

216
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:08,680
And again, actually, for this banning pre-existing conditions

217
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:11,200
clauses, it's actually a more specific version

218
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:13,200
of something that is also very popular,

219
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:15,960
which is equal rates for everybody.

220
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:17,640
Equal rates for everybody.

221
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:19,240
Life insurance when you're 105.

222
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:21,160
That should be just as much as life insurance

223
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:22,880
for when you're 25, right?

224
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:24,200
That's fair.

225
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:26,920
Well, he's not using your fault, you're 105.

226
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:29,240
Why are the fat cats being so mean?

227
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:32,280
Why can't they just be cool about this?

228
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:34,520
All right, anyway.

229
00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:39,560
So this is a nice description of the way

230
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:41,920
that the regulation is supposed to be together.

231
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:44,160
And then, as you may recall, there

232
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:46,160
was a Supreme Court case trying to overturn

233
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:47,920
the mandate, which failed.

234
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,040
But then, let's see, actually, so at least

235
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:52,600
in one version of the latest Trump tax plan,

236
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:54,160
the mandate was abolished.

237
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:56,400
Does anyone know what happened to the final version?

238
00:09:56,400 --> 00:09:59,920
Did it end up, was it really killed or not?

239
00:09:59,920 --> 00:10:01,320
I think it was killed in the bill.

240
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:02,200
I don't know if it's.

241
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:03,920
Yes, but like which bill?

242
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:05,120
I think the one that passed.

243
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:06,120
In the final version?

244
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:07,720
So it was removed in the final version?

245
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:08,240
I think so.

246
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:09,240
OK.

247
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:10,160
Right.

248
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:11,480
So again, that sounds terrible.

249
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:15,520
Now, the other thing to remember is that the actual,

250
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:17,360
the mandate is not a true mandate,

251
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:20,960
because if you didn't want to do it, you would just pay a fine.

252
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:24,400
Which is how the Supreme Court wound up approving it.

253
00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:26,640
Because they said, well, really, it's just like a tax.

254
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:29,060
If you don't do it, then you have to pay something for it.

255
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:31,320
But the original tax was quite small anyway.

256
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:34,000
So really, the only way to argue in favor of the package

257
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,760
was to say, well, this is just a temporary low fine.

258
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:40,760
But once we have the principle of a fine around,

259
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:43,520
then we can go and jack it up to a level

260
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:46,400
enough to actually get rid of the incentive

261
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:51,720
to just wait until you need the insurance.

262
00:10:51,720 --> 00:10:52,320
OK.

263
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:54,160
So questions about this?

264
00:10:56,920 --> 00:10:59,680
Anyway, so praise the Krugman for actually laying this out

265
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:02,400
clearly and explaining it in detail,

266
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:05,600
rather than just going and putting smiley faces

267
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:08,840
on things, which is what most people at the time did.

268
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:10,800
My question's not exactly about this,

